  "query_preview": "across the region from the west-southwest. Record warmth is \npossible next week with latest guidance suggesting several areas of \n100+ temps Monday/Tuesday and with little to no shower/thunderstorm \nactivity. Conditions still remain hot heading through mid-week as \nwell with humid conditions. Additional heat headlines are likely\nearly into the middle of next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Strong to severe thunderstorms are possible this \nafternoon and Sunday afternoon.\n\nThe heat and humidity (mentioned in Key Message 1) will provide a \nmoderate amount of instability across our area this afternoon and \nagain Sunday afternoon. Depending on the model, SBCAPE values peak \nin the 2,000-2,500 J/kg range, with DCAPE values upwards of 800 \nJ/kg. There are some minor differences amongst the various near-term \nCAMS and even the synoptic models. But the general consensus is that \nthere should be isolated to scattered convection both afternoons. \nThe storms are most likely to start along the sea breeze and then \nshift inland, with outflow and boundary interactions playing a role \nin storm formation and persistence. Given the aforementioned \ninstability, strong to severe thunderstorms are possible. The main \nthreat would be damaging winds. But heavy rainfall is also a concern \ndue to weak steering flow. Backbuilding and/or training of storms \ncould drop enough rainfall to generate very localized flooding. The \nconvection each afternoon is expected to dissipate each evening.",
